1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,080
You've  spent  the  last  few  lessons

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:03,879
learning  who  you  are,  what

3
00:00:03,879 --> 00:00:05,600
strengths  you  bring  and  why

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,080
different  people  make  a  team

5
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:08,400
stronger?

6
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,360
Now,  we  can  name  the  skill  that

7
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,000
turns  those  ideas  into  action.

8
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,320
Leadership.  The  source  curriculum

9
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:16,720
gives  us  a  simple  working

10
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:17,960
definition.

11
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,880
Leadership  is  directing  people

12
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:21,640
toward  a  goal.

13
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:23,119
Notice  what  that  definition  does

14
00:00:23,119 --> 00:00:25,760
not  say.  It  does  not  say  having

15
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:28,400
a  title,  being  the  loudest  person

16
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,359
or  giving  orders.

17
00:00:30,359 --> 00:00:32,200
Leadership  is  about  helping  people

18
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:33,680
move  towards  something  they  are

19
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:36,320
trying  to  achieve.  For  a  community

20
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,360
leader,  the  first  question  is  not

21
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,920
am  I  important?  It  is  can  I  help

22
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:43,375
people  move  toward  a  useful  goal?

23
00:00:43,375 --> 00:00:45,175
The  curriculum  also  makes  an

24
00:00:45,175 --> 00:00:47,734
important  point.  Leadership  is  not

25
00:00:47,734 --> 00:00:49,335
reserved  for  one  special

26
00:00:49,335 --> 00:00:51,655
personality.  Its  message  is  that

27
00:00:51,655 --> 00:00:53,335
leadership  can  be  acquired  and

28
00:00:53,335 --> 00:00:55,575
developed.  Some  people  may  feel

29
00:00:55,575 --> 00:00:57,054
naturally  comfortable  taking  the

30
00:00:57,054 --> 00:00:58,775
lead  while  others  build  that

31
00:00:58,775 --> 00:01:01,095
ability  through  experience,  practice

32
00:01:01,095 --> 00:01:02,734
and  responsibility.

33
00:01:02,734 --> 00:01:04,855
That  means  you  should  not  decide

34
00:01:04,855 --> 00:01:06,775
I'm  just  not  a  leader  because  you

35
00:01:06,775 --> 00:01:09,255
are  quiet,  careful,  creative  or

36
00:01:09,255 --> 00:01:11,535
people  focused.  Those  traits  can

37
00:01:11,535 --> 00:01:13,734
all  appear  in  leadership.

38
00:01:13,734 --> 00:01:15,654
The  better  question  is,  how  do  I

39
00:01:15,654 --> 00:01:17,895
naturally  influence  a  group  and

40
00:01:17,895 --> 00:01:19,495
what  leadership  skills  do  I  still

41
00:01:19,495 --> 00:01:21,135
need  to  strengthen?

42
00:01:21,135 --> 00:01:22,855
Community  leadership  grows  through

43
00:01:22,855 --> 00:01:24,934
practice,  not  through  waiting  for

44
00:01:24,934 --> 00:01:25,708
a  title.

45
00:01:25,708 --> 00:01:28,188
leadership  can  also  look  different

46
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:30,668
from  one  situation  to  another.

47
00:01:30,668 --> 00:01:32,467
Some  people  lead  by  giving  clear

48
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:34,068
direction  and  making  fast

49
00:01:34,068 --> 00:01:35,387
decisions.

50
00:01:35,387 --> 00:01:37,307
Others  persuade  people  around  an

51
00:01:37,307 --> 00:01:38,548
idea.

52
00:01:38,548 --> 00:01:40,868
Some  motivate  a  team,  while  others

53
00:01:40,868 --> 00:01:42,308
become  role  models  through

54
00:01:42,308 --> 00:01:44,067
consistent  actions  and  the  way

55
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:45,708
they  treat  people.

56
00:01:45,708 --> 00:01:47,588
None  of  those  methods  automatically

57
00:01:47,588 --> 00:01:49,588
makes  one  person  more  of  a  leader

58
00:01:49,588 --> 00:01:51,067
than  another.

59
00:01:51,067 --> 00:01:52,708
The  source  curriculum  wants  you  to

60
00:01:52,708 --> 00:01:54,948
recognize  that  different  situations

61
00:01:54,948 --> 00:01:56,987
need  different  strengths.

62
00:01:56,987 --> 00:01:59,028
A  useful  leader  learns  how  they

63
00:01:59,028 --> 00:02:01,068
tend  to  lead,  understands  how

64
00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:03,107
other  people  lead  and  chooses  the

65
00:02:03,107 --> 00:02:04,628
approach  that  helps  the  group

66
00:02:04,628 --> 00:02:06,587
reach  the  goal  without  ignoring

67
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:07,874
the  people  involved.

68
00:02:07,874 --> 00:02:10,033
The  curriculum  uses  a  teamwork

69
00:02:10,033 --> 00:02:12,394
video  with  penguins,  ants  and

70
00:02:12,394 --> 00:02:14,114
other  animals  to  make  one

71
00:02:14,114 --> 00:02:16,194
leadership  lesson  visible.  The

72
00:02:16,194 --> 00:02:17,714
strength  of  a  group  can  be

73
00:02:17,714 --> 00:02:19,473
greater  than  the  strength  of  one

74
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:21,034
individual.

75
00:02:21,034 --> 00:02:22,513
Pause  the  video  and  watch  the

76
00:02:22,513 --> 00:02:24,954
linked  teamwork  clip.

77
00:02:24,954 --> 00:02:26,993
When  you  return,  focus  on  one

78
00:02:26,993 --> 00:02:29,233
question.  What  made  the  smaller

79
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:31,714
individuals  successful?

80
00:02:31,714 --> 00:02:33,434
The  lesson  is  not  that  everyone

81
00:02:33,434 --> 00:02:35,434
must  act  the  same.  It  is  that

82
00:02:35,434 --> 00:02:37,393
people  become  more  effective  when

83
00:02:37,393 --> 00:02:38,313
they  under  understand  their

84
00:02:38,313 --> 00:02:40,674
abilities,  cooperate  and  combine

85
00:02:40,674 --> 00:02:42,034
strengths.

86
00:02:42,034 --> 00:02:43,714
A  community  leader  should  develop

87
00:02:43,714 --> 00:02:46,113
personal  skills,  but  also  know  how

88
00:02:46,113 --> 00:02:47,913
to  bring  different  people  together

89
00:02:47,913 --> 00:02:49,394
so  those  skills  can  support  one

90
00:02:49,394 --> 00:02:50,082
another.

91
00:02:50,441 --> 00:02:52,122
Now,  you'll  use  the  official

92
00:02:52,122 --> 00:02:53,922
leadership  type  assessment  provided

93
00:02:53,922 --> 00:02:55,322
with  this  lesson.

94
00:02:55,322 --> 00:02:56,481
The  source  explains  that  the

95
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:57,961
assessment  contains  groups  of

96
00:02:57,961 --> 00:02:59,882
traits  that  you  rank  according  to

97
00:02:59,882 --> 00:03:01,801
how  strongly  they  describe  you.

98
00:03:01,801 --> 00:03:03,442
Then  total  the  results  to  see

99
00:03:03,442 --> 00:03:05,042
which  leadership  patterns  appear

100
00:03:05,042 --> 00:03:06,482
most  strongly.

101
00:03:06,482 --> 00:03:08,402
Follow  the  instructions  on  the  ACT

102
00:03:08,402 --> 00:03:10,802
worksheet  exactly.  Do  not  try  to

103
00:03:10,802 --> 00:03:12,802
force  the  result  you  want.

104
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:14,441
Pause  the  video  and  complete  the

105
00:03:14,441 --> 00:03:16,562
assessment  honestly.

106
00:03:16,562 --> 00:03:18,121
Treat  the  result  as  information

107
00:03:18,121 --> 00:03:20,121
about  your  current  tendencies.  Not

108
00:03:20,121 --> 00:03:21,841
What  is  a  permanent  label?

109
00:03:21,841 --> 00:03:23,321
The  next  lesson  will  explain  the

110
00:03:23,321 --> 00:03:25,242
four  leadership  directions  and  help

111
00:03:25,242 --> 00:03:26,641
you  understand  what  your  result

112
00:03:26,641 --> 00:03:28,457
actually  means  in  practice.

113
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:30,817
Before  you  leave  the  assessment,

114
00:03:30,817 --> 00:03:33,137
remember  what  a  result  can  and

115
00:03:33,137 --> 00:03:34,816
cannot  tell  you.

116
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:36,537
It  can  help  you  notice  patterns

117
00:03:36,537 --> 00:03:39,096
in  how  you  think,  communicate,

118
00:03:39,096 --> 00:03:41,697
decide  or  work  with  people.

119
00:03:41,697 --> 00:03:43,657
It  cannot  define  your  whole

120
00:03:43,657 --> 00:03:46,097
identity  and  it  does  not  prove

121
00:03:46,097 --> 00:03:47,736
that  one  leadership  style  is

122
00:03:47,736 --> 00:03:49,617
better  than  another.

123
00:03:49,617 --> 00:03:51,457
The  source  is  very  clear  that

124
00:03:51,457 --> 00:03:53,656
different  leadership  types  matter

125
00:03:53,656 --> 00:03:55,617
because  different  situations  need

126
00:03:55,617 --> 00:03:57,457
different  qualities.

127
00:03:57,457 --> 00:03:58,457
A  result  should  create

128
00:03:58,457 --> 00:04:00,097
self-awareness,

129
00:04:00,097 --> 00:04:01,856
not  a  box.

130
00:04:01,856 --> 00:04:04,176
If  two  patterns  seem  close,  that

131
00:04:04,176 --> 00:04:06,777
is  not  a  problem.  Human  behavior

132
00:04:06,777 --> 00:04:08,457
changes  across  roles  and

133
00:04:08,457 --> 00:04:10,777
situations,  and  you  will  understand

134
00:04:10,777 --> 00:04:12,137
that  better  when  we  build  the

135
00:04:12,137 --> 00:04:13,777
leadership  compass  in  the  next

136
00:04:13,777 --> 00:04:14,498
lesson.

137
00:04:14,778 --> 00:04:16,858
There's  one  more  source  activity

138
00:04:16,858 --> 00:04:18,658
worth  keeping  because  it  gives  you

139
00:04:18,658 --> 00:04:20,618
a  different  kind  of  evidence  about

140
00:04:20,618 --> 00:04:21,857
yourself.

141
00:04:21,857 --> 00:04:23,818
Choose  five  people  whose  opinions

142
00:04:23,818 --> 00:04:26,418
you  trust  and  ask  each  one  two

143
00:04:26,418 --> 00:04:27,618
questions.

144
00:04:27,618 --> 00:04:29,858
What  are  my  strengths  and  what

145
00:04:29,858 --> 00:04:32,338
positive  traits  do  you  see  in  me?

146
00:04:32,338 --> 00:04:34,098
Your  goal  is  to  collect  several

147
00:04:34,098 --> 00:04:36,338
specific  answers,  including  repeated

148
00:04:36,338 --> 00:04:37,178
ones.

149
00:04:37,178 --> 00:04:38,978
If  the  same  strength  appears  more

150
00:04:38,978 --> 00:04:41,058
than  once,  that  may  be  a  useful

151
00:04:41,058 --> 00:04:42,658
pattern  to  notice.

152
00:04:42,658 --> 00:04:44,537
But  remember,  these  answers  are

153
00:04:44,537 --> 00:04:47,018
opinions,  not  final  facts  about

154
00:04:47,018 --> 00:04:48,377
who  you  are.

155
00:04:48,377 --> 00:04:49,897
Pause  now  to  write  the  names  of

156
00:04:49,897 --> 00:04:51,618
the  five  people  you  will  ask,

157
00:04:51,618 --> 00:04:53,178
then  complete  the  conversations

158
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:54,706
before  the  next  lesson.

159
00:04:55,105 --> 00:04:57,186
Why  ask  other  people  when  this

160
00:04:57,186 --> 00:04:59,626
unit  is  about  self-awareness?

161
00:04:59,626 --> 00:05:01,226
Because  leaders  need  both  an

162
00:05:01,226 --> 00:05:04,265
internal  view  and  an  external  one.

163
00:05:04,265 --> 00:05:06,265
You  know  your  intentions,  but

164
00:05:06,265 --> 00:05:08,026
other  people  experience  your

165
00:05:08,026 --> 00:05:09,265
behavior.

166
00:05:09,265 --> 00:05:11,186
Their  feedback  can  reveal  strengths

167
00:05:11,186 --> 00:05:13,106
you  overlook  or  show  that  a

168
00:05:13,106 --> 00:05:15,186
quality  you  value  is  visible

169
00:05:15,186 --> 00:05:17,266
consistently  to  others.

170
00:05:17,266 --> 00:05:19,386
At  the  same  time,  one  person's

171
00:05:19,386 --> 00:05:21,866
opinion,  especially  a  negative  one,

172
00:05:21,866 --> 00:05:24,745
does  not  define  you.  Use  feedback

173
00:05:24,745 --> 00:05:27,026
as  information  to  examine,  not  a

174
00:05:27,026 --> 00:05:29,105
verdict  to  obey.

175
00:05:29,105 --> 00:05:30,425
The  curriculum  connects  this

176
00:05:30,425 --> 00:05:32,866
research  with  development.  Discover

177
00:05:32,866 --> 00:05:34,585
your  strengths  so  you  can  invest

178
00:05:34,585 --> 00:05:36,226
in  them.  Keep  developing

179
00:05:36,226 --> 00:05:38,306
undiscovered  abilities  and  become

180
00:05:38,306 --> 00:05:39,906
more  useful  to  the  people  and

181
00:05:39,906 --> 00:05:41,497
projects  around  you.

182
00:05:41,497 --> 00:05:43,617
Today,  you  learned  that  leadership

183
00:05:43,617 --> 00:05:45,497
is  a  skill  that  can  be  developed,

184
00:05:45,497 --> 00:05:47,256
not  one  personality  you  either

185
00:05:47,256 --> 00:05:49,577
have  or  do  not  have.  You  learned

186
00:05:49,577 --> 00:05:50,737
that  people  can  lead  through

187
00:05:50,737 --> 00:05:53,377
direction,  influence,  motivation,

188
00:05:53,377 --> 00:05:55,417
example,  and  teamwork,  and  that

189
00:05:55,417 --> 00:05:57,176
knowing  your  own  tendencies  helps

190
00:05:57,176 --> 00:05:59,417
you  contribute  more  deliberately.

191
00:05:59,417 --> 00:06:00,936
You  also  completed  a  leadership

192
00:06:00,936 --> 00:06:02,536
assessment  and  began  collecting

193
00:06:02,536 --> 00:06:03,776
outside  feedback  about  your

194
00:06:03,776 --> 00:06:06,497
strengths.  Keep  both  results.  In

195
00:06:06,497 --> 00:06:07,977
the  next  lesson,  we  place  those

196
00:06:07,977 --> 00:06:09,337
patterns  onto  the  leadership

197
00:06:09,337 --> 00:06:11,297
compass,  explore  four  different

198
00:06:11,297 --> 00:06:13,096
leader  ship  directions  and  ask  a

199
00:06:13,096 --> 00:06:14,857
question  every  community  leader

200
00:06:14,857 --> 00:06:16,857
needs  to  answer.  When  is  my

201
00:06:16,857 --> 00:06:18,937
natural  style  useful  and  when  do

202
00:06:18,937 --> 00:06:20,122
I  need  to  adapt?

